--   Finite face partitions, refinements, and pullbacks
-- statement:
--   A face partition is a finite partition of the whole finite face space. Its complexity is its number of genuine atoms, and a representative may be chosen from each nonempty atom.
--
--   The indiscrete partition has one ambient cell when the space is nonempty. Common refinement is supplied for two partitions and for a finite family, with the empty family giving the indiscrete partition. A finite family of cuts generates the partition whose atoms have the same membership bits for every cut. A map pulls a target partition back by declaring two points equivalent when their images lie in the same target atom. The underlying partition order runs from finer to coarser.
